From f9509260cf58d8eb348f58f82a74ef27ed7df6b9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Klochek <christopher.klochek@sentry.io>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 14:21:18 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Bound recursion depth in the parser
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

swc_ecma_parser parses expressions, and nested statements, with mutual
recursion whose depth is driven entirely by the input. The crate's only
stacker::maybe_grow call site covers the consequent of an if statement, so
nesting is otherwise bounded by the thread stack and a few kilobytes of
hostile source exhausts it. A stack overflow is a SIGSEGV/abort in Rust
rather than an unwind, so it cannot be caught and takes the process down.

On a 2 MiB stack the stock parser aborts on, among others, new(()=>…) at
depth 297, ({a:…}) at 357, (…) at 625, nested blocks at under 5_000, prefix
operator chains at ~4_500, and chained ternaries at ~8_760.

Adding maybe_grow to those paths is not on its own a fix: it moves the stack
onto fresh heap segments rather than capping it, which converts the abort
into unbounded memory growth. Measured with maybe_grow alone, nesting cost
~3.3 KB of RSS per level, an amplification of roughly 1700x from input bytes:
a 781 KB input reached 1_329 MB of RSS, and a few megabytes of input would
exhaust any host. That is a cheaper denial of service than the crash it
replaces.

So bound the depth and report a syntax error past the limit, with maybe_grow
alongside it so that the permitted depth is comfortably reachable on a small
stack. Exceeding the limit is now an ordinary SyntaxError::TooMuchRecursion,
which callers can recover from and which flows through swc's existing
recoverable-error machinery.

Guards are placed at the four entry points of the recursion:

  parse_assignment_expr                 brackets, ternaries, binary and comma
                                        chains, arrow function bodies
  parse_unary_expr                      prefix operator chains
  parse_member_expr_or_new_expr_inner   new expression chains
  parse_stmt_internal                   nested blocks and function bodies

MAX_RECURSION_DEPTH is 2048. The deepest of the JavaScript fixtures in
getsentry/symbolic is jquery.js at 54, so this leaves roughly 38x headroom.

Verified:

  - Behaviour-preserving. ASTs are identical to the stock crate's for all 27
    JavaScript fixtures in getsentry/symbolic.
  - Bounded. A 781 KB nested payload now peaks at 6 MB of RSS and a graceful
    parse error, against 1_329 MB with maybe_grow alone and an abort on stock.
  - No performance cost. jquery.min.js parses in 9.2 ms vs 9.7 ms stock.
  - Sequence expressions are parsed iteratively and are unaffected: a 2 MB
    comma chain costs 91 MB on both stock and this build.

Note that dropping a deeply nested AST is also recursive. The depth limit
keeps ASTs well clear of the depth at which that matters, but a caller that
raises MAX_RECURSION_DEPTH substantially should re-check it.
